Israel has freed a palestinian prisoner  who'd been on a 56-day hunger strike in  protest at his detention it's the tenth  time hudner Adnan had been detained  without charge by the Israelis we asked  london-based Palestinian journalists  Sharif and Nash hibi why he'd been  released I think his rods decision to  release him was more to do with the the  risk of upheaval if he died because  human rights groups have been  criticizing Israel's treatment of  Palestinian prisoners for many years and  that hasn't really had any effect on  Israeli policy towards Palestinian  prisoners so I think it was more to do  with with fears of the security  repercussions of him dying in prison  Adnan has consistently denied any  involvement in violence and the ten  times that he's been in prison he's not  been charging this is a fundamental  problem of Israel's prisoner policy is  that it can hold Palestinians  indefinitely without charge without due  process on the horrific conditions they  make accusations but if Israel has the  evidence to back up these accusations  then these prisoners should be charged  and should have a fair trial if a  certain party is not willing to be  transparent in its actions we cannot  take its allegations at face value there  are activists and militants out there  who are proud of their actions in their  involvement in whatever they do but he  has consistently denied involvement in  violence and without evidence to the  contrary then I don't think his word is  any less believable than Ezra it's  probably even more believable in the  absence that only evidence in the  confident the scale of Israel's  administrative detention policy is huge  and we have to remember that although  Adnan's release is currently being  celebrated in the occupied territories  there are several thousand palestinians  languishing in Israeli jails facing  terrible conditions that include torture  and these are long been highlighted by  international and Israeli human rights  groups so the scale of the issues should  not be forgotten amid the celebrations  of the release of any particular  prisoner particularly in light of the  fact that Israel has a revolving door  policy where it can release a prisoner  only to detain them once again on more  than one occasion this has to be borne  in mind this is not an Israeli  concession this is part of a very  problem of Israeli detection and  treatment of Palestinians without charge  without due process and severe  mistreatment which includes Palestinian  children one can very easily condemn the  kidnapping of people and also the  illegal imprisonment of people they're  not mutually exclusive so I don't think  by condemning Israel's attention policy  one can assume that that someone  supports the kidnapping of people both  are wrong but on the scale of things  Israel's detention of Palestinian  prisoners is a much larger issue this  issue long proceeds any kidnappings in  Gaza so while both are wrong we cannot  say well one is justified because the  other happens there they are both  condemnable
 

